				\begin{document}$$\mu_{o}$$\end{document}</tex-math><mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" id="M26"><mml:msub><mml:mi>μ</mml:mi><mml:mi>o</mml:mi></mml:msub></mml:math><inline-graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="41598_2021_84763_Article_IEq10.gif"><?image-name 41598_2021_84763_Article_IEq10.gif?><?image-size 186?><?image-md5 d464c3af728e149e35ebdfb6f814c7db?><?image-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?image-scaled-height 29?><?image-scaled-width 20?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/6c8c/7970878/d464c3af728e/41598_2021_84763_Article_IEq10.gif?><?thumb-name 41598_2021_84763_Article_IEq10.gif?><?thumb-size 186?><?thumb-md5 d464c3af728e149e35ebdfb6f814c7db?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 29?><?thumb-scaled-width 20?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/6c8c/7970878/d464c3af728e/41598_2021_84763_Article_IEq10.gif?></inline-graphic></alternatives></inline-formula>. Supplementary Table <xref rid="MOESM1" ref-type="media">S2</xref> collects the physical parameters of rocks and fluid for calculation, where we assume the frame rock of granite and saturating fluid of andesitic melt for imaged magma reservoir (L1, Fig. <xref rid="Fig3" ref-type="fig">3</xref>). The calculation then yields a relationship of the V<sub>p</sub> reduction and porosity (i.e. melt fraction) as shown in Fig. <xref rid="Fig5" ref-type="fig">5</xref>. So, given the V<sub>p</sub> reduction of 19% for the magma reservoir from characteristic-model tests in Supplementary Fig. <xref rid="MOESM1" ref-type="media">S10</xref>, the melt fraction is estimated to be ~ 19%.</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="supplementary-material"><title>Supplementary Information</title><sec id="Sec12"><p>
